Lab 1: Introduction to Red Hat Fuse Architecture
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will:

Understand the core components of Red Hat Fuse (Camel, Karaf, ActiveMQ, and Fuse Tooling).
Learn how these components interact in a cloud-native, hybrid IT environment.
Review common use cases and integration scenarios.
Prerequisites
Basic understanding of integration concepts and Java.
A Linux-based environment (Ubuntu 20.04+ recommended).
Podman or Docker installed (for containerized deployment).
OpenJDK 11+ installed.
Access to a terminal/command line.
Lab Tasks
Task 1: Explore Core Components of Red Hat Fuse
Subtask 1.1: Introduction to Apache Camel
Apache Camel is an open-source integration framework that implements Enterprise Integration Patterns (EIPs).

Steps:

Install Camel dependencies using Maven:
mvn archetype:generate -DarchetypeGroupId=org.apache.camel.archetypes -DarchetypeArtifactId=camel-archetype-java -DarchetypeVersion=3.20.0
Navigate to the generated project and run:
cd camel-java-project
mvn camel:run
Observe the Camel context starting up. Expected output:
[INFO] Started CamelContext: camel-1
Key Concept: Camel routes define integration flows between systems.

Subtask 1.2: Explore Apache Karaf
Karaf is a lightweight OSGi container used in Red Hat Fuse.

Steps:

Download Karaf:
wget https://archive.apache.org/dist/karaf/4.4.0/apache-karaf-4.4.0.tar.gz
tar -xvf apache-karaf-4.4.0.tar.gz
cd apache-karaf-4.4.0/bin
./karaf
Verify OSGi bundles in the Karaf shell:
bundle:list
Install a Camel feature:
feature:install camel
Expected Outcome: Karaf console with active OSGi bundles.

Subtask 1.3: Configure ActiveMQ
ActiveMQ is a messaging broker for asynchronous communication.

Steps:

Run ActiveMQ in a container:
podman run -d --name activemq -p 61616:61616 -p 8161:8161 rmohr/activemq
Access the ActiveMQ web console at http://localhost:8161 (credentials: admin/admin).
Create a test queue named test.queue.
Troubleshooting: If ports are blocked, use podman ps to verify the container status.

Subtask 1.4: Fuse Tooling in VS Code
Red Hat Fuse Tooling provides IDE support for integration projects.

Steps:

Install VS Code and the "Red Hat Fuse Tooling" extension.
Create a new Camel project:
File > New → Red Hat Fuse → Camel Project.
Design a sample route using the visual editor.
Key Concept: Fuse Tooling simplifies route development with drag-and-drop.

Task 2: Interaction in a Cloud-Native Environment
Subtask 2.1: Deploy Fuse on OpenShift
Log in to OpenShift:
oc login --server=<your-cluster-url>
Deploy a Fuse integration using S2I:
oc new-app --name=my-fuse-app https://github.com/syndesis-quickstarts/rest-http-api
Expected Outcome: Fuse application running in a containerized environment.

Task 3: Review Use Cases
Subtask 3.1: File Integration Scenario
Create a Camel route to move files:
from("file:/input?noop=true")
  .log("Processing file: ${header.CamelFileName}")
  .to("file:/output");
Place a test file in /input and verify it appears in /output.
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Explored Red Hat Fuse components (Camel, Karaf, ActiveMQ, and Tooling).
Deployed Fuse integration in a cloud-native environment.
Reviewed real-world use cases.
Next Steps
Experiment with advanced Camel EIPs (e.g., splitter, aggregator).
Explore Fuse on OpenShift with Knative for serverless integrations.
Troubleshooting Tips
For Karaf issues, check logs in apache-karaf-4.4.0/data/log/.
Use oc logs <pod-name> for OpenShift debugging.
